      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nLISA S. BLATT, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nMALCOLM L. STEWART, Deputy Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.;\nfor the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nDAVID D. COLE, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BLATT: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nTinker should apply off-campus for three reasons. First, such speech can cause on-campus disruption. Second, Respondents' approach would create chaos. And, third, a school nexus requirement and Tinker's substantial disruption test guard against abuse."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Ms. Blatt, it seems to me that your argument that Tinker is the entire analysis may depend on a --on a version of Tinker that the lower courts really have not adopted, because you say that there can't be any regulation of political or reg -- or regulation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. BLATT",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BLATT: Right. These are all -- these are all fair points and what was argued in the district court. I will say the district court said it was pretty much fatal to Tinker that she said it off campus."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Malcolm L. STEWART",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Under the Third Circuit's approach, B.L. could send out Snaps from her home every evening disparaging the coaches, her teammates, and the enterprise of cheerleading."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF MALCOLM L. STEWART FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE PETITIONER"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What about speech -- political speech that involves the school, you know, opposing a school referendum because Ms. Jones is a terrible teacher? Does -- do you categorize that as political speech,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- when we talk about material disruption as a basis for preventing or disciplining students who engage in certain speeches or conduct, can you also localize that to the team so that if a team member disrupts the team, that it's okay to discipline that team member, even though you wouldn't normally do it, discipline that person as a member of the student body?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "cheerleaders or student-athletes will do things that disrupt the operations of the team but don't have any larger effect on the school as a whole."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stewart",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the three or four things I would say are, first, no per se rule that off-campus speech is categorically exempt from school regulation. Second, with respect to online speech in particular, the location from which the -- the post is sent is more or less irrelevant to the likelihood that it would cause disruption. Third, a school can permissibly conclude, as in the team concept -- context, that particular speech will disrupt the operations of a particular school program even if it doesn't disrupt the operations of the school as a whole. And, fourth, in determining what counts as substantial disruption, we should look to the purposes of the program."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Mr. Cole, that sharp line I think you're trying to draw between on campus and off campus, how does that fit with modern technology? I mean, it's -- it's -- if a text or a Snap that you send, you send from the -- the park and it's read in the cafeteria, is that off campus or -- or on campus?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: So, if you're -- what the -- our test is the test that this Court applied in Morse versus Frederick, which is, if you are under the school's supervision or sanction, the school has the authority that the school speech cases give it. And if you're outside of the school's supervision or sanction, then you -- the -- the same First Amendment rights apply to you as apply to everybody else."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "B.L. was punished for merely expressing frustration with a four-letter word to her friends outside of school on a weekend."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: The Internet doesn't -- the Internet doesn't change that, Your Honor. The Internet -- if anything, the Internet underscores the importance of assuring that kids outside of school have the right to speak freely because that's where kids speak. They -- they speak to their friends, they share their most intimate thoughts, on the Internet with their friends. If any time they do that and -- and -- and that means that somebody in school at some point might read it, the school can, therefore, regulate it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the school's supervision, the school has broad authority based on disruption alone. But outside of the school --outside of school supervision, the First Amendment governs, and the school has the same authority that the city would have with respect to regulating speech that is not under its supervision."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So do you -- does the speech -- MR. COLE: Our first -- JUSTICE THOMAS: -- does the speech occur when -- and I'm sorry to interrupt you, but does it occur when it's written or posted or when it's read or downloaded?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 83,
        "start": 83
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Is what?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 83,
        "start": 83
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What's aggressive about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel (Mr. Cole)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0004-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 83,
        "start": 83
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Absolutely not. The -- the school would be permitted to regulate that conduct if it satisfies a First Amendment, permissible definition of bullying."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel (Mr. Cole)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0004-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Your Honor, within the school context, all they have to find is that it's disruptive."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 104,
        "start": 104
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Well, I think it's very much worth the candle."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 104,
        "start": 104
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: No, not at all. Not at all. I -- I think -- look, there are hundreds of Tinker cases. Virtually all of them involve suspensions for things that kids do vis-\u03b1-vis as students. There's about a handful of -- of -- of -- of team cases. So that's a different category."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 105,
        "start": 105
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Mr. Cole, I want to pick up where Justice Kavanaugh left off. I want to make sure I understand your approach. Let's talk about the harassment example that you were discussing with Justice Sotomayor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 108,
        "start": 108
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: So -- so -- so Giboney allows for the prohibition of speech integral to prohibited conduct, and -- and -- and that covers aiding and abetting. So aiding or abetting cheating is just not protected at all under Giboney."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COLE: Yes, because you would be -- you would be subjecting yourself to the school's jurisdiction. If you're -- if you call the school, if you send an e-mail to the school account, you are now subjecting yourself to the jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cole",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 111,
        "start": 111
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Everyone agrees off-campus bullying, harassment, and threats properly defined can be regulated. The difference between the other side's test and ours is this: Ours would protect political speech, whistleblowing, and venting frustration outside school even if a principal predicts it will lead to disruption."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dea4cd024cd1-0005-0009",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 115,
        "start": 115
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's no case law on conduct that aids and abets school speech. You will have a school speech petition. You can keep denying cert, but I guarantee the courts are going to -- they're going to freak out when Tinker has been the law off campus for 20 years."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-20-255",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "dea4cd024cd1c96f3addaa940136f9c5229d89b18fc1106a7ee2a24a5d0525f8",
